# Reference CODEOWNERS for a Bicep module registry repo. Rename to `CODEOWNERS` and place at
# the repo root (or `.github/CODEOWNERS`) to activate it — GitHub/Azure DevOps then requires
# a review from the matched group before a PR touching that path can merge.
#
# The split below is the actual governance decision this whole experiment is about: the
# contract surface (shared/, foundation/) requires platform sign-off; everything built on top
# of it (workload/) does not, because it structurally cannot violate the contract without
# `bicep build` or PSRule already failing the PR first.

# Contract vocabulary — changing a shared type changes every module that imports it.
/shared/                          @contoso/platform-engineering

# Platform-owned modules — these wrap AVM and decide what squads are allowed to set.
/foundation/                      @contoso/platform-engineering

# Squad-contributed shared modules. Lighter than /foundation/ — reviewed by a rotating
# platform+squad pool, not the full platform team. governance/rfc-template.md requires an
# RFC to exist before this PR is opened; governance/contributed-module-checklist.md is the
# review bar. Give a specific module its own line below once it has a clear owning team,
# so changes route to that team directly instead of the generic pool.
/contributed/                     @contoso/module-reviewers
# /contributed/service-bus-namespace/  @contoso/messaging-squad

# Pipeline gates and policy-as-code config — changing what's *checked* is as sensitive as
# changing what's *allowed*.
/bicepconfig.json                 @contoso/platform-engineering
/ci/                               @contoso/platform-engineering
/governance/                       @contoso/platform-engineering

# Squad-authored composition, private to one team — normal team review, no platform gate.
/workload/                        @contoso/squad-leads
